Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: realtek: honour realtek,hsgmii on RTL8221B SerDes

The FiberHome AN4475-G2 wires the RTL8221B to the Airoha EN7581 ETH PCS and
describes the link as phy-mode = "2500base-x".  Mainline then programs
MMD 30.0x697a[5:0] = 2, i.e. standard 2500BASE-X, while the EN7581 PCS
driver selects AIROHA_SCU_ETH_XSI_HSGMII for PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_2500BASEX
and calls airoha_pcs_init_hsgmii(); it never enables standard 2500BASE-X.

The two ends therefore speak different protocols on the same 3.125G lane:
the PHY's copper side links normally, but the host PCS receives nothing.

  pcs_pma DIG_RO_RESERVE_2 RX_SIGDET = 0/6     (PON PCS, same method: 6/6)

Datasheet 8.6.1 defines 30.0x697a[5:0] as: 0 = 2500Base-X+SGMII,
1 = HiSGMII+SGMII, 2 = 2500Base-X, 3 = HiSGMII.  Mainline has no HiSGMII
constant at all, so a board in this situation cannot express the mode it
needs through phy-mode alone.

Add the missing mode and honour an explicit realtek,hsgmii devicetree
property, the same way other Realtek-based boards do.  Boards that do not
set the property keep the existing behaviour.

Note 30.0x6a04[10:8] is already programmed to 3'b101 (HiSGMII/2500BASE-X)
by the existing magic-number sequence, and rtl822xb_get_rate_matching()
returns RATE_MATCH_NONE for any mode other than 2, which is what HiSGMII
wants.

Signed-off-by: local

--- a/drivers/net/phy/realtek/realtek_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/realtek/realtek_main.c
@@ -132,6 +132,7 @@
 #define RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_OPTION_MODE_MASK		GENMASK(5, 0)
 #define RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_OPTION_MODE_2500BASEX_SGMII		0
 #define RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_OPTION_MODE_2500BASEX		2
+#define RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_OPTION_MODE_HSGMII			3
 
 #define RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_CTRL3			0x7580
 #define RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_CTRL3_MODE_MASK		GENMASK(5, 0)
@@ -1999,6 +2000,17 @@
 		phydev->rate_matching = RATE_MATCH_NONE;
 	}
 
+	/* Some hosts call their 3.125G port "2500base-x" in DT but run
+	 * Realtek HiSGMII on the wire: Airoha EN7581 selects
+	 * AIROHA_SCU_ETH_XSI_HSGMII for PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_2500BASEX and
+	 * never enables standard 2500BASE-X. Programming mode 2 there
+	 * leaves the host PCS with no received signal at all.
+	 */
+	if (device_property_read_bool(&phydev->mdio.dev, "realtek,hsgmii")) {
+		mode = RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_OPTION_MODE_HSGMII;
+		phydev->rate_matching = RATE_MATCH_NONE;
+	}
+
 	/* the following sequence with magic numbers sets up the SerDes
 	 * option mode
 	 */
